  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1148 AM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nA compact, northern stream shortwave trough will dive \nsoutheastward across the Central Plains into the Ozarks on \nThursday. High-resolution guidance and convective ensembles \ncontinue to show moisture return ahead of this feature will be \nlimited, with PWAT values maxing out between 0.75 and 1.0 inch. \nThe NBM maintains a wide range of PoPs (20-70%) Thursday, with \nhighest chances mainly confined to east-central Oklahoma and \ngenerally west of Highway 75 in northeast Oklahoma Thursday \nafternoon. Instability is expected to be very limited, MUCAPE \nmainly less than 300 J/kg, but an isolated rumble of thunder or\ntwo cannot be completely ruled out, especially across east- \ncentral Oklahoma in the afternoon. Rainfall accumulations are \nforecast to be on the light side, with consensus in guidance \nshowing accumulations anywhere from a few hundredths of an inch \nto one third of an inch possible. A reinforcing cold front will \nsweep through behind the passing upper-wave Thursday afternoon and\nevening, turning winds back out of the northwest and ushering in \nanother shot of cool, dry air.\n\nFor Friday through the weekend, global guidance and ensembles are\nin strong alignment on expansive mid-level ridging building \nacross the central CONUS. Surface high pressure settles over the \narea Friday and Saturday, crisp fall-like mornings with lows \ngenerally in the mid-to-upper 30s and lower 40s through the \nupcoming weekend. A few areas may even dip to or just below \nfreezing, especially Saturday night/Sunday morning. Highs will \nhold in the upper 50s to mid 60s through the weekend before \nmoderating into the upper 60s and lower 70s by early next week.\n\nAs the ridge axis slides eastward early to mid-week next week, \nsustained southerly return flow will become re-established across \neastern Oklahoma and northwest Arkansas. This will foster a \ngradual warming trend, with afternoon highs climbing into the low-\nto-mid 70s Tuesday and next Wednesday. Model consensus remains \ndry CWA-wide through the extended forecast window, with no \nsignificant weather hazards or organized storm systems on the \nhorizon.\n\nMejia",
